# Target C-II — repeated exact-swap blockers have `D^-2` slack and row-parallel direction

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only strengthening of the one-sign strong-TB1 exact-swap system; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** one-sign strong-TB1 exact swaps through `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8687`  
**Dependencies:** common strong-TB1 swap axis; exact contact-swap algebra; disjoint physical path layers; row-parallel almost-diameter packing lemma  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_tb1_exact_swap_double_witness_collapse_t18687.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The existing double-witness theorem was stated for the contact-minimal **overswap**. The Horn system, however, is encoded using the exact contact swaps themselves. The same collapse is true at the exact-swap endpoint and follows from the same two-interface algebra with the overswap factor removed.

Let a previously strict source pair block the singleton exact swap at two distinct one-sign strong-TB1 interfaces `i,j`. Then the two physical-layer incidences orient the same pair oppositely. The exact swap axes satisfy

\[
|w_i-w_j|=O(D^{-1}),
\]

and the exact swap lengths satisfy

\[
q_i,q_j=O(D^{-1}).
\]

If the pair hits the lower metric boundary at both swaps, then

\[
\boxed{|xy|-1=O(D^{-2})}.
\tag{ED.1}
\]

If it hits the diameter boundary at both swaps, then

\[
\boxed{D-|xy|=O(D^{-2})}.
\tag{ED.2}
\]

In both cases its projective direction lies within `O(D^-1)` of the common train row line. Consequently the source-wide row-parallel packing lemma gives

\[
\boxed{
N_{\rm repeated,UPPER}^{\rm exact}=O(1).
}
\tag{ED.3}
\]

This supplies the exact-swap version needed by the all-residue Horn UPPER deduplication theorem. No appeal to overswap persistence is required.

---

## 1. Exact-swap notation and opposite layer incidence

At one-sign interface `i`, write the exact contact swap as

\[
v_i=-q_iw_i,
\qquad
q_i=|a_i+b_i|>0,
\qquad
|w_i|=1.
\tag{ED.4}
\]

The common-axis theorem gives fixed constants `Q,W` such that, uniformly over the strong-TB1 population,

\[
q_i\le {Q\over D},
\qquad
|w_i-w_j|\le {W\over D}
\tag{ED.5}
\]

for all sufficiently large `D`. The current explicit bound permits `Q=31.4\times10^6+o(1)`; its value is immaterial here.

Let `h=xy` be moved by two distinct physical layers `R_i,R_j`. Since the layers are disjoint and the pair has two endpoints, one endpoint lies in each layer. Orient

\[
r=p_y-p_x,
\qquad
\ell=|r|,
\]

and let `n` be the inward relative vector at interface `i`. Then the inward vector at `j` is exactly `-n`:

\[
\boxed{n_j=-n_i=-n.}
\tag{ED.6}

At interface `i`, the post-swap squared length is

\[
\ell_i'^2
=\ell^2-2q_i(n\cdot w_i)+q_i^2.
\tag{ED.7}
\]

At `j`, because the incidence sign reverses,

\[
\ell_j'^2
=\ell^2+2q_j(n\cdot w_j)+q_j^2.
\tag{ED.8}

Put

\[
d_{ij}:=|w_i-w_j|,
\qquad
H_{ij}:={q_iq_j\over q_i+q_j}.
\tag{ED.9}

Then

\[
d_{ij}=O(D^{-1}),
\qquad
H_{ij}=O(D^{-1}).
\tag{ED.10}

No positive lower bound on either `q_i` or `q_j` is used.

---

## 2. Repeated exact LOWER blockers have `O(D^-2)` slack

Assume

\[
\ell>1,
\qquad
\ell_i'\le1,
\qquad
\ell_j'\le1.
\tag{ED.11}

Set

\[
A:=\ell^2-1>0.
\]

Equation (ED.7) gives

\[
n\cdot w_i
\ge {A+q_i^2\over2q_i}
={A\over2q_i}+{q_i\over2}.
\tag{ED.12}

Equation (ED.8) gives

\[
n\cdot w_j
\le-{A+q_j^2\over2q_j}
=-{A\over2q_j}-{q_j\over2}.
\tag{ED.13}

Subtracting and using

\[
|n\cdot(w_i-w_j)|\le\ell d_{ij}
\]

gives

\[
\ell d_{ij}
\ge
{A\over2}\left({1\over q_i}+{1\over q_j}\right)
+{q_i+q_j\over2}.
\tag{ED.14}

Multiply by `2H_ij`:

\[
\boxed{
A+q_iq_j
\le2\ell d_{ij}H_{ij}.
}
\tag{ED.15}

Every exact LOWER blocker already satisfies

\[
1<\ell\le1+q_i=1+O(D^{-1}),
\]

so `ell=O(1)`. Together with (ED.10), (ED.15) gives

\[
A=O(D^{-2}).
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\ell-1={A\over\ell+1}=O(D^{-2}).
}
\tag{ED.16}

The direction also collapses. Equation (ED.12) makes `n dot w_i>0`, while (ED.13) makes `n dot w_j<0`. Hence

\[
0<n\cdot w_i
\le|n\cdot(w_i-w_j)|
\le\ell d_{ij}=O(D^{-1}).
\tag{ED.17}

After division by `ell=1+o(1)`, the direction of the pair is `O(D^-1)` from the line perpendicular to the common swap axis. This is the common train row line.

---

## 3. Repeated exact UPPER blockers have `O(D^-2)` diameter slack

Assume instead

\[
\ell<D,
\qquad
\ell_i'\ge D,
\qquad
\ell_j'\ge D.
\tag{ED.18}

Put

\[
B:=D^2-\ell^2>0.
\]

Equation (ED.7) yields

\[
n\cdot w_i
\le {q_i\over2}-{B\over2q_i},
\tag{ED.19}

and (ED.8) yields

\[
n\cdot w_j
\ge {B\over2q_j}-{q_j\over2}.
\tag{ED.20}

Thus

\[
\ell d_{ij}
\ge
{B\over2}\left({1\over q_i}+{1\over q_j}\right)
-{q_i+q_j\over2}.
\tag{ED.21}

Multiplication by `2H_ij` gives

\[
\boxed{
B-q_iq_j
\le2\ell d_{ij}H_{ij},
}
\tag{ED.22}

hence

\[
\boxed{
B\le2\ell d_{ij}H_{ij}+q_iq_j.
}
\tag{ED.23}

Every exact UPPER blocker already lies in the local window

\[
D-q_i\le\ell<D,
\]

so `ell=D-O(D^-1)`. Using (ED.10) in (ED.23),

\[
2\ell d_{ij}H_{ij}=O(D^{-1}),
\qquad
q_iq_j=O(D^{-2}),
\]

and therefore

\[
B=O(D^{-1}).
\]

Since

\[
D-\ell={B\over D+\ell},
\]

we obtain

\[
\boxed{D-\ell=O(D^{-2}).}
\tag{ED.24}

For the direction, (ED.19) implies

\[
n\cdot w_i\le q_i/2=O(D^{-1}),
\]

while (ED.20) and the axis difference give

\[
n\cdot w_i
\ge n\cdot w_j-\ell d_{ij}
\ge -q_j/2-\ell d_{ij}
=O(1)\text{ from below}.
\]

Consequently

\[
\left|{n\over\ell}\cdot w_i\right|=O(D^{-1}),
\tag{ED.25}
\]

because `ell~D`. With `w_i=w_*+O(D^-1)`, the projective direction `[xy]` is `O(D^-1)` from the common row line.

---

## 4. Repeated exact UPPER blockers are source-wide bounded

Sections 1--3 give fixed constants `A,L` such that every source pair which blocks the exact UPPER boundary at two distinct one-sign strong-TB1 interfaces satisfies

\[
D-{A\over D^2}\le|xy|<D
\tag{ED.26}

and

\[
d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}([xy],[u_*])\le {L\over D}.
\tag{ED.27}

The already proved row-parallel almost-diameter packing lemma is purely metric: in a unit-separated planar source of diameter `D`, the total number of pairs satisfying (ED.26)--(ED.27) is `O_{A,L}(1)`.

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
N_{\rm repeated,UPPER}^{\rm exact}=O(1).
}
\tag{ED.28}

Unlike the repeated-LOWER matching statement, no chosen-witness uniqueness is needed for this count. Equation (ED.28) applies to **all** repeated exact UPPER blockers in the source.

---

## 5. Horn consequence

A Horn UPPER blocker is a previously strict source pair which is bad under one singleton exact swap. This includes

- permanent UPPER forbidden pairs;
- persistent two-coordinate UPPER pairs;
- and UPPER pairs realizing implication arcs.

If the same source pair appears in UPPER extractions from two different modulo-six Horn systems, it is bad under singleton exact swaps at two distinct physical layers. Hence it is counted by (ED.28).

Thus only `O(1)` source pairs can duplicate across the six UPPER classwise extractions. This is exactly the missing justification for removing the factor-two UPPER deduplication loss in the all-residue Horn theorem.

The analogous conclusion is false for LOWER: Sections 1--2 put a repeated exact LOWER pair into an ultra-near-unit row-parallel class, but a linear vertex-disjoint family of such pairs has not been excluded.

---

## 6. Frontier effect

The exact finite-swap system and the earlier overswap analysis now have the same multiplicity-two metric collapse:

```text
same strict pair blocks two one-sign layer moves
    -> opposite endpoint-incidence signs
    + swap axes differ by O(D^-1)
    + swap magnitudes are O(D^-1)

    -> same active boundary forces D^-2 original slack
    + pair direction lies in the common row cone.
```

For UPPER, the source-wide row-parallel near-diameter packing theorem then makes repetition bounded. This converts all-residue UPPER occurrence counts into distinct-pair counts up to only `O(1)` loss.

**Disposition:** proved the exact-swap double-witness collapse and the source-wide `O(1)` bound for repeated exact UPPER blockers. C-II and Level I remain open.
